-- Adds 1 new Module 2 lesson filling a gap found by comparing this academy
-- against DeepLearning.AI/Karpathy/CS50 AI's early ML-fundamentals material:
-- this curriculum taught train/test splitting but never validation sets,
-- hyperparameters as a named concept, or regularization -- all standard
-- "week 1-2" material in every one of those courses.
--
-- Anchored to a lesson TITLE via subquery, not a hardcoded order_index, so
-- this is safe to run regardless of what other renumbering migrations have
-- already been applied. Placed right after "When AI Gets It Wrong" (problem
-- diagnosed, then how you actually guard against it) as the new close of
-- Module 2. Same negate-then-restore trick as prior renumbering migrations;
-- lesson_progress/lesson_quiz_questions key off lesson_id, never order_index.

UPDATE public.lessons SET order_index = -order_index
WHERE order_index > (SELECT order_index FROM public.lessons WHERE module_number = 2 AND title = 'When AI Gets It Wrong');
UPDATE public.lessons SET order_index = -order_index + 1 WHERE order_index < 0;

WITH new_lesson AS (
  INSERT INTO public.lessons (module_number, order_index, title, slug, summary, coin_cost, is_published)
  VALUES (
    2,
    (SELECT order_index FROM public.lessons WHERE module_number = 2 AND title = 'When AI Gets It Wrong') + 1,
    'Validation Sets & Hyperparameter Tuning',
    'validation-sets-and-hyperparameter-tuning',
    'The third data split that keeps your final test score honest -- and the choices you actually get to tune with it.',
    10,
    true
  )
  RETURNING id
), ins_content AS (
  INSERT INTO public.lesson_content (lesson_id, content)
  SELECT id, '{"hook":"Every time you use your TEST set to decide ‘let''s try a different learning_rate,’ you''ve secretly let your test set influence training — and now it can''t honestly tell you how the model will do on data it''s truly never seen.","explanation":"So far you''ve split data into train and test. But there''s a subtle trap: if you keep checking your TEST set every time you tweak something (try a different learning_rate, add a feature, change model size) and pick whatever scores best on it, you''re indirectly tuning YOUR CHOICES to that specific test set — the same overfitting problem, just one level up. The fix is a THIRD split: a VALIDATION set. Train on the training set as normal. After each attempt, check performance on the validation set, and use THAT to decide what to adjust. Only touch the test set once, right at the very end, for an honest final score. The things you adjust between attempts — learning_rate, how many training epochs, how complex the model is — are called HYPERPARAMETERS: choices YOU make before training starts, as opposed to the model''s own weights, which training itself adjusts. One common hyperparameter is regularization strength — a dial that discourages the model from fitting the training data TOO closely, directly fighting overfitting. Tuning hyperparameters using the validation set, never the test set, is what keeps your final test score honest.","analogy":"It''s like practicing for an exam with practice tests (validation set) to figure out which study method works — you can look at as many practice tests as you want. The REAL exam (test set) you only take once, and never see the questions in advance, or the score stops meaning anything.","fun_fact":"This is sometimes called ‘test set leakage’ — even well-known research teams have been caught unintentionally overfitting to a popular public test set just by re-checking it too many times over the years.","try_it":"You''re tuning a model and it''s tempting to just check the test set ‘one more time’ after every small tweak. Explain in your own words why that habit quietly ruins the test set''s whole purpose.","code":"# A 3-way split: train, validation, test\ndataset_size = 100\ntrain_end = int(dataset_size * 0.7)\nval_end = int(dataset_size * 0.85)\n\ntrain_set = list(range(0, train_end))           # 70%: used to fit the model\nval_set   = list(range(train_end, val_end))      # 15%: used to compare attempts\ntest_set  = list(range(val_end, dataset_size))   # 15%: touched ONCE, at the very end\n\nprint(f\"Train: {len(train_set)}, Validation: {len(val_set)}, Test: {len(test_set)}\")\n\n# Trying different hyperparameters -- ONLY checked against the validation set\nhyperparameter_attempts = [\n    {\"learning_rate\": 0.1, \"val_score\": 0.72},\n    {\"learning_rate\": 0.01, \"val_score\": 0.89},   # best so far\n    {\"learning_rate\": 0.001, \"val_score\": 0.81},\n]\nbest = max(hyperparameter_attempts, key=lambda a: a[\"val_score\"])\nprint(f\"Best learning_rate based on VALIDATION score: {best[''learning_rate'']}\")\n# Only NOW, with the winning choice locked in, would you check test_set -- once.","visual":{"caption":"Three splits, three separate jobs","steps":[{"emoji":"🏋️","label":"Train Set","caption":"Used to actually fit the model’s weights."},{"emoji":"🧪","label":"Validation Set","caption":"Used to compare attempts and tune hyperparameters."},{"emoji":"🔁","label":"Repeat & Tune","caption":"Try different hyperparameters, checking validation each time."},{"emoji":"🔒","label":"Test Set","caption":"Touched exactly once — the final, honest score."}]},"practice":{"prompt":"Why should you avoid repeatedly checking your TEST set while tuning hyperparameters?","options":["Doing so indirectly overfits your choices to that specific test set, making its final score dishonest","The test set physically deletes itself after one use","It has no real effect either way","Test sets can only be checked by validation sets, never by humans"],"correct_index":0,"feedback":"This is exactly why a validation set exists — to absorb all the repeated checking during tuning, so the test set stays untouched until one final, trustworthy check."}}'::jsonb FROM new_lesson
  RETURNING lesson_id
)
INSERT INTO public.lesson_quiz_questions (lesson_id, order_index, question, options, correct_index, explanation)
SELECT ins_content.lesson_id, q.order_index, q.question, q.options::jsonb, q.correct_index, q.explanation
FROM ins_content, (VALUES
  (1, 'What is a validation set used for?', '["Comparing different attempts and tuning hyperparameters, without touching the test set","Training the model’s weights directly, same as the training set","Nothing — only train and test sets are ever needed","Storing backup copies of the training data"]', 0, 'The validation set absorbs the repeated checking that happens while tuning choices, keeping the test set untouched until the end.'),
  (2, 'What is a hyperparameter?', '["A choice made before training starts, like learning_rate, rather than something the model learns on its own","Another name for the model’s trained weights","A synonym for the test set","A type of neural network activation function"]', 0, 'Hyperparameters are decisions a person makes (learning rate, model size, etc.) — the weights themselves are what training actually learns.'),
  (3, 'Why is it a problem to repeatedly check the TEST set while trying different hyperparameters?', '["It indirectly tunes your choices to that specific test set, the same overfitting problem one level up","Test sets get smaller every time they are checked","It is not actually a problem at all","It makes training run slower each time"]', 0, 'Picking whichever hyperparameters score best on repeated test-set checks quietly overfits your decisions to that test set.'),
  (4, 'In the code example, which learning_rate was chosen, and why?', '["0.01, because it had the highest VALIDATION score among the attempts tried","0.1, because it was tried first","0.001, because smaller numbers are always better","All three were used at the same time"]', 0, '0.01 scored 0.89 on the validation set, the highest of the three attempts — exactly the number used to pick a winner.'),
  (5, 'What is regularization, as introduced in this lesson?', '["A hyperparameter that discourages a model from fitting the training data too closely, fighting overfitting","A way to make training run without any data at all","A technique that only applies to image models","A synonym for the test set"]', 0, 'Regularization strength is one common hyperparameter, tuned via the validation set, specifically aimed at reducing overfitting.'),
  (6, 'When should the test set actually be checked?', '["Once, at the very end, after all hyperparameter tuning is already finished","After every single training epoch","Before the model has even been built","Continuously, in real time, throughout training"]', 0, 'The test set’s entire value comes from being checked exactly once, after every decision has already been locked in using the validation set.'),
  (7, 'A team keeps adjusting their model based on test set performance, then reports that same test score as their "final result." What is wrong with this?', '["The reported score is no longer honest — the test set was used for tuning, not just final evaluation","Nothing is wrong — this is exactly the correct process","The model is now guaranteed to work perfectly in the real world","This process only matters for very large datasets"]', 0, 'Once a test set has influenced any decision-making, it can no longer honestly measure how the model performs on truly unseen data.')
) AS q(order_index, question, options, correct_index, explanation);
